Looking for audio visual rental for weddings in Malaga? This guide is not the place for the Wedding Pack's price or its guest capacity, our [wedding rentals](/blog/wedding-rentals/) guide covers that in full. This page covers something different: what actually happens with the sound and the microphones across your wedding day, from the soundcheck before your first guest is seated to the moment the reception turns into dancing, including the one complication most other events on our books never have to plan for, a ceremony and a reception in two different spaces, often one outdoor and one indoor, inside the same booking.

Contact us today to discuss your ceremony and reception, whatever the two spaces look like.

## Brief Overview

---

A wedding is not one static AV setup, it is the same PA system, wireless microphones and technician working across several different moments in one day: a quiet soundcheck, a ceremony built around one or two voices, speeches that pass a microphone hand to hand, a reception that settles into ambient background sound, and a dancefloor that needs real volume. The [Wedding Pack](/packages/wedding/) covers all of it for up to 80 guests, but what changes hour to hour is how that equipment gets used, not which equipment shows up.

This guide walks through that day in order, ending with the one piece of it that is genuinely unique to a wedding: the switch between your ceremony space and your reception space, inside a single event.

## Key Highlights

---

-   A soundcheck happens before your first guest is seated, testing the actual venue rather than an empty room hours earlier.
-   A wireless handheld from the Wedding Pack's wireless microphones is the standard setup for the vows: no cable to trip over, and the officiant can step away from the stand.
-   The Wedding Pack is the only package in our catalog with a technician for the full event and no hour cap, watching levels and feedback from the vows to the last dance.
-   A hands free lavalier for the officiant is possible as a separate, quote based item, not part of the package.
-   Speeches use the same wireless handheld microphone, with our technician managing the handoff between speakers so there is no dead air.
-   Fairy lights and ambient lighting for the reception are the simple half of the day: setup and cabling, not a technical process.

<InlineCTA />

## Before the Ceremony: Soundcheck and Setup

Our crew arrives and runs a soundcheck ahead of your first guest being seated, testing the officiant's microphone and the ceremony speakers against the actual space rather than an empty room hours earlier. This is the wedding day version of the same testing our crew runs at every booking. For the deeper explanation of that process itself, gain staging and how an indoor hall and an open air ceremony get set up differently, see our Audio System Calibration guide elsewhere on this blog.

## During the Vows: The Officiant's Microphone, and Why It Needs a Technician

For the vows themselves, the standard setup is one of the wireless handheld microphones included in the [Wedding Pack](/packages/wedding/), held by the officiant: no cable across the aisle, and no need for anyone to stay behind a stand during your own vows. If the officiant needs both hands free, to read from a book or to hold the rings, a wireless lavalier clipped to the collar is possible as a separate, quote based item, not a package inclusion.

The honest tradeoff is worth saying plainly: any microphone run through a live PA system outdoors or in a small hall can feed back, the rising squeal a system makes when it picks up its own amplified sound, and a lavalier worn close to the body is more prone to it than a handheld. That is why the [Wedding Pack](/packages/wedding/) includes a technician on site for the entire event, not a set number of hours, the only package in our catalog built that way (the MICE Pack technician covers up to 6 hours). Our technician watches that risk during your vows and adjusts before it ever reaches your guests, rather than after.

## Speeches at the Reception: The Handheld Microphone and the Handoff

Once your ceremony ends and speeches begin, the same wireless handheld microphone moves to the reception, passed from speaker to speaker or set on a stand at the head table. As the best man, the maid of honor or a parent takes their turn, our technician manages the handoff between speakers so a beat of silence never turns into a long, awkward pause while a microphone gets passed and repositioned.

## The Ceremony to Reception Switch: When Your Venue Changes Mid Event

This is the part of a wedding day that no other event on our books has to plan for. A corporate conference or a birthday party runs in one room, calibrated once. A wedding, very often, does not: an outdoor ceremony under a floral arch moves into an indoor or covered reception once the vows are done, or the reverse, an indoor ceremony followed by an open air reception.

Our crew already adjusts sound continuously as your day moves from ceremony to speeches to dancing, the same real coverage described on our wedding rentals guide. When that also means a change of room and not just a change of moment, the same principle applies: a room's acoustics change what correct settings look like, whether that room change happens between two separate bookings or in the middle of one wedding day. That is exactly why the Wedding Pack technician has no hour cap: the day can call for more than one calibrated setup, not just one long one held from a single position.

## Fairy Lights and Ambient Sound Once the Tables Are Set

Once your reception space is ready, the [Wedding Pack](/packages/wedding/) adds fairy lights and warm LED strings for ambient lighting, with professional setup and cabling run cleanly rather than left as a trip hazard across the floor. This is intentionally the simple half of the day: no technical process to explain, just lighting placed and powered correctly ahead of your guests arriving at their tables.

## Dancing: When the Volume Changes

The last shift in the day is the most straightforward: as dinner and speeches give way to dancing, our technician brings the sound up from the conversational level speeches need to the level a dancefloor actually needs, then keeps adjusting it as the room fills or empties through the night.

## What This Guide Does Not Cover

This page is strictly about the wedding day's own AV arc, not the package, its price or its full inclusions. See our [wedding rentals](/blog/wedding-rentals/) guide for that, along with booking lead time and service area. It is also not the generic explanation of gain staging, indoor versus outdoor acoustics, or equipment troubleshooting: those are covered in general terms, for any event type, in our Audio System Calibration, Audio Visual Rental for Outdoor Events, Technical Support for Events and AV Technician Hire guides elsewhere on this blog. And it is not decor, furniture or dancefloor accessories: the Wedding Pack does not include those, and neither does this page.

